0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700">Good morning, world. </span><span=
>Not that long ago, the Strait of Hormuz was something I never really thoug=
ht about. Now I write about it all the time. And suddenly we=E2=80=99re all=
 obsessed with another strategic strait that until recently most of us coul=
dn=E2=80=99t place on a map: the Bab al-Mandab.</span></p><p style=3D"font:=
normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>It connect=
s the Red Sea and the Gulf of Aden and has been the main workaround for Sau=
di Arabia=E2=80=99s oil exports since the closure of the Strait of Hormuz. =
But as of last week, it was claimed by the Houthis, an Iranian-backed milit=
ia.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;ma=
rgin:0 0 15px"><span>The Houthis are on a roll. They have intermittently ma=
de headlines over the years for disrupting shipping in the Red Sea and for =
their alliance with Iran. And now they=E2=80=99ve become meaningful players=
 in a conflict that is hurting economies around the world. We are likely to=
 hear a lot more about them. So who are they=3F And what exactly do they wa=
nt=3F I spoke to my colleague Vivian Nereim, our Gulf bureau chief, to find=
 out.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;=
