p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15p=
x"><span>It=E2=80=99s a really bad scenario for them. Their last war agains=
t the Houthis was a disaster in every way =E2=80=94 for humanitarian reason=
s, for their reputation, for their finances =E2=80=94 and they didn=E2=80=
=99t even achieve their goals. They basically lost, and the Houthis have on=
ly become more powerful since. Now they have to decide: Do they get togethe=
r a coalition to fight back=3F The U.S. has pretty much said it won=E2=80=
=99t get involved.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,seri=
f;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Saudi Arabia is supposed to host the Wo=
rld Cup in 2034. It=E2=80=99s hosting the World Expo in 2030. It really jus=
t needs to get the Houthis under control and nothing has worked.</span></p>=
<p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"=
><span style=3D"font-weight:700">So, zooming out, what does this mean for t=
he Middle East and the Iran war=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/2=
5px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>In a way, Saudi Arabia=
=E2=80=99s situation is emblematic of the broader Gulf dilemma: What do the=
y do now=3F They can=E2=80=99t really take on Iran head-on. They=E2=80=99re=
 not strong enough. The U.S. has become as much of a liability as a help, b=
ut they also don=E2=80=99t have any alternatives.</span></p><p style=3D"fon=
t:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>That lea=
ves diplomacy as the only real option, but diplomacy right now is kind of h=
umiliating for them given that Iran has been attacking them for months.</sp=
an></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 =
0 15px"><span>One major lesson of the Houthis is the extent to which geogra=
